I can remember growing up as a gamer my  friends and I would always talk about  the leap in graphics from one generation  to the next I mean anytime a new console  came out the first thing we always  talked about is can you believe how good  the graphics are now don't get me wrong  graphics alone don't make a video game  as we all know but it's almost always  the very first thing you notice right  the graphics the art style how it looks  and with Ratchet and Clank Rift apart I  really do feel like games have taken a  huge step forward particularly in this  animated Style  foreign  and while they're probably not quite on  the level of something like a Pixar film  just yet with Rift apart I do feel like  they are getting ridiculously close at  this point as you'll see here in this  video I mean even if you aren't A PS5  owner just as a fan of animation and  gaming I think even PC Gamers Xbox fans  will no doubt appreciate the absolutely  gorgeous visuals and just jaw-dropping  animation work from Insomniac here now  movies are always going to have an  inherent Advantage because filmmakers  are able to control every shot and sure  games can do this in cut scenes but  video game animators also have to  account for one crucial difference  between video games and movies and  that's of course us a player we have the  freedom to explore the world and analyze  nearly every single texture every detail  and then they need to have all of that  running at a smooth frame rate so the  actual gameplay is enjoyable so one  thing Rift apart really has going for it  is that it's one of the few games  developed from the ground up for PS5 and  only PS5 even other first party titles  such as Horizon forbidden West master  god of war Ragnarok while they look  fantastic they also have PS4 versions as  well so they weren't developed from the  ground up directly for the PS5 there are  a variety of different graphical modes  here in Ratchet and Clank and I ended up  going with the performance Ray tracing  mode on a 4K OLED television and let me  tell you even the performance mode looks  absolutely spectacular and I think to  fully appreciate this game you need a 4K  HDR TV to just appreciate everything  they've got going here now I'm not  saying it looks bad at 1080 but I did  try and capture some gameplay while  playing on a 1080 Monitor and while  everything looked good it just didn't  have that wow factor that I experienced  when playing it directly on the 4K TV so  the footage you're seeing here is 1080  but it's captured directly from my PS5  and depending on the size of screen that  you're watching it on if you're watching  it on a smaller screen it's probably  going to look great but if you're  watching it on a big TV or even a large  monitor don't let that fool you this  game in full 4K resolution is just so  nice my eight-year-old son has actually  been getting into this game a little bit  as well he saw me playing and thought it  looked so fun so he's tried it out a  little bit and we just keep talking  about I mean how it looks like a movie  of course being a parent we're a big fan  of Pixar here and again thinking about  all those inherent advantages that  movies have in terms of being able to  control the shot and the set frame rate  even with all of those considerations I  still especially in cutscenes I mean  Ratchet just comes insanely close the  reflections on Clank alone are  astounding I mean every time it showed  Clank at a cut scene I just could not  get over how glorious everything looked  reflecting off of him  okay  so you were being honest as I have been  saying repeatedly now I'm not a super  technical guy I don't know all the bells  and whistles and what's truly going on  here but I assume it is incredibly  difficult to do I know digital Foundry  has done a full breakdown on all the  graphical modes so if you really want to  get into the nitty-gritty there with all  the graphics highly recommend their  stuff as far as the frame rate goes for  me personally I found it to be a very  smooth experience with only the  occasional slowdown and that came during  some late game sections that were really  kind of intense sections of gameplay but  I would say 95 of the time the game felt  very smooth to me and again I was  playing on the performance Ray tracing  mode so I think it's pretty clear for me  the graphics are just absolutely  phenomenal but is rift apart purely a  visual showcase or is there more here to  make this title worth your time so I  just recently finished up my first  playthrough of the game and actually  even decided to go for the platinum  trophy and enjoyed every minute of it I  thought it was fantastic throughout I  have to say one of the common criticisms  that I've seen and kind of read online  for Rift apart is that it's just too  short and that makes me a little afraid  to admit that I beat the game over the  course of about four months now before  you immediately click away from the  video and say this guy is the Worst  Gamer I've ever seen or heard of it  wasn't four months of consistent play  typically it was an hour or two here and  there when I had time between being a  dad husband working my full-time job and  of course working on this YouTube  channel and as a kid or a teenager even  I probably would have totally just flew  through this game and been like oh that  was too short and honestly may have even  forgotten it a lot faster but the older  I've gotten and just given the fact that  I have less time to really put into  those long gaming sessions I've started  to appreciate more of these games that  are a much more contained experience and  games that have this kind of innate  approachability where you can jump out  and jump back in later without feeling  like you've just completely forgotten  how to play the game you're totally lost  in the story I think it's why I've  tended to gravitate more toward  multiplayer games in recent years  because I think you know they're so pick  up and play you can jump in at any time  but I have to say for Ratchet and Clank  Rift apart even as a single player game  I think one of the area is it really  shines is in just how approachable it is  even though I played it over the course  of months sometimes only having time to  play once a week I rarely ever felt like  I had to relearn something when I jump  back in there was this one particular  move with these kind of launch pads that  you find throughout the game I  completely forgot how to do that after  it had been a particularly long break  between sessions but that's the only  thing everything else was just  immediately familiar to me immediately  came back to me and it controls great  the mechanics and movement work well it  has a nice slick menu system and more  than anything it's just plain fun I mean  at the end of the day what I kept coming  back to with ratchet is just how  enjoyable it is to sit down and play the  game Traverse through the different  worlds searching for all the  Collectibles such as the gold bolts plus  all of the various weapon upgrades and  speaking of the weapons this has always  been kind of a staple of the ratchet  Series where they really shine as in all  of these zany bizarre over-the-top  weapons and most of them are pretty good  here there are a few that I never cared  for or really wanted to use much but I'd  say roughly three-fourths of them are  actually worth using or at least offer  some sort of fun element that make them  enjoyable to use and the game also makes  it super easy to control and switch  between the weapons you can map your  favorite 4 to the d-pad but most of the  time I found myself just going straight  to the weapon wheel all you have to do  is hold down the triangle you get the  full weapon wheel and at first you just  have one weapon wheel but the more you  go through the game and unlock more  weapons you end up having I think it's  like three weapon Wheels by the end so  at that point when you have that many  weapons it is kind of like an extra step  to page over but for the most part this  system just works really well now going  into this game one of the most talked  about features hyped up quite a bit by  Sony actually was this idea of being  able to transport immediately into other  dimensions using riffs hence of course  the name Ratchet and Clank ripped apart  now this was certainly visually  impressive and I can't imagine what all  it took under the hood to actually make  that work but overall it honestly didn't  have a huge impact on Gameplay at least  for me not as much as I was expecting  anyway I anticipated quite a few clever  puzzles and maybe some intriguing  gameplay scenarios taking advantage of  this kind of bouncing back and forth but  it really ends up being far more simple  than that it's almost just like you're  transported to another world for a bit  and then you jump back to the other  world I mean there are some times when  they use the dimension hopping mechanics  to have you start in one dimension then  you like hit a crystal transport to  another then you can reach an area that  you couldn't in the other dimension then  you progress there hit a crystal then  you're back in the other dimension that  sort of thing things that we've seen  before now to be fair there are some of  the more fast-paced sections with  Dimension hopping that I won't spoil  here I'm not even going to show them  because those are just absolutely  stunning visual I mean it's a visual  treat to see those but gameplay wise it  kind of just left me wanting a bit more  in that regard there are also a couple  of different puzzle type sections  throughout the game one features a  little Nano systems it bot named glitch  and another featuring Clank now for the  glitch sections I just did not care much  for these at all there's nothing really  bad about them they're just not as fun  as the core game and nothing really  Innovative about them I get there kind  of intended to be a change of pace but  it really just didn't work for me the  Clank puzzle sections on the other hand  though were great I found these often  struck that perfect balance between just  challenge enough to get you to have to  think a little bit but not so incredibly  difficult that you're just frustrated  and want to put the game down now one of  my favorite sections of the game are  these little challenges that you unlock  they take place in a battle arena on  what I think is the scar stew debris  field area these were pretty enjoyable  and some of them are actually quite  challenging and even required me several  attempts to beat they really forced you  to kind of stay on your toes during  combat and to take advantage of all the  different evasion mechanics that you  have in this game to really in some  cases keep from getting hit now one  little tip here I'll say if you're  struggling with any of these if you're  going for the platinum trophy or  something and you want to do everything  you can always come back to these after  you've leveled up certain weapons or  even your health a bit more there are  some for example that will require you  to just use one specific weapon and now  if you don't have that weapon upgraded  very high it's going to be even harder  but if you go back out Traverse the  other areas level up that weapon and  then come back it'll be much easier so  if you're ever looking for a more  challenging section that gives you a  break from the story then I would  definitely check these out now speaking  of the story though the characters in  Rift apart are fantastic mean the  Ratchet and Clank those two characters  for sure are iconic at this point you  already know them as the main two Heroes  but this game also introduces us to  rivet she is a lombax From Another  Dimension as well as kit now kit is  essentially clank's dimensional  counterpart and serves most of the same  gameplay functions as Clank but she does  have a few surprises in store as it  relates to the story and I'm not gonna  spoil those here for you so you will  play as both ratchet and rivet and  receive assistance from Clank and kit  throughout the game that's not a huge  spoiler there as far as the gameplay  between the different characters it's  exactly the same which I would expect  some will like and others may be  disappointed by personally I didn't mind  it too much I appreciated that I had all  the weapons and upgrades that I unlocked  no matter which character I was on I  would have access to all of them but in  terms of that differentiation sort of a  different feel between characters it's  just purely a visual difference and then  of course the different story segments  as well they are exactly the same to  control now while the characters are  great here the story in Rift apart is  just kind of there really I mean it's  not as basic as something like I don't  know the 3D Mario games but it's it's  pretty close to that level and honestly  does Ratchet and Clank need to have some  amazing incredible story no I don't  think so they could honestly stick with  the exact same formula they've been  using and be fine but if there was an  area they could look to improve I would  certainly add a better story to the list  we were talking about Pixar a bit in the  beginning and so while the game visually  is getting pretty close from a story  standpoint is far short of what you're  gonna find in a even Pixar's weaker  stories and as I was kind of writing  this video I think this is also an area  where the industry as a whole could  improve I mean if you think about most  of the amazing video game stories that  come to mind I don't know maybe just  because I'm older but the ones that come  to mind for me are more mature titles  there's like Red Dead Redemption Persona  the last of us there are so many of  those to count I mean too many to count  when it comes to more adult games but  I'd love to see some developers really  put some effort behind making a  top-notch Pixar level story from a more  family-friendly game series such as  ratchet I mean I've tried to sit here  right now and think of some good  examples of a family-friendly game with  a great story and honestly I'm  struggling most of the great family  friendly games are purely focused on  gameplay I mean maybe something like  undertale if you guys can think of any  let me know in the comments I'd love to  check them out with my sons so for me  ratchet and colleague is one of those  games I'd be fine playing once or twice  every new console generation updated  visuals updated weapons a new story The  Core mechanics they're just simply that  good they're plain fun and as games  continue to just grow bigger and bigger  and bigger and layer on all these  complex systems don't get me wrong I  love some of that stuff but at the end  of the day it's kind of a good reminder  to come back to just a traditional old  school video game and even though  ratchet is 3D and didn't come around  until the PS2 era I still think of it as  kind of an old school game at this point  now I do also have to admit there's part  of me that wonders if Insomniac really  wanted to take things to the next level  with ratchet like if they said hey let's  really level this up and go all out  there's nothing wrong with playing it  safe don't get me wrong I mean most  reviewers love drift apart it sold well  especially for a new system I personally  enjoyed it immensely all that being said  it's a series that would no doubt  benefit from a full open world treatment  it really would even more insane weapons  more Collectibles and even though it  wouldn't be at the top of my list I  would at least like to see them attempt  a more fleshed out story because again  the characters themselves are actually  quite good so is Ratchet and Clank worth  your time absolutely if you're a fan of  third person shooters action and a  family-friendly setting with lovable  characters I highly recommend it now if  you'd like to see a more extensive list  of PS5 games I'd recommend for families  and kids check out the video on the  right side of your screen now as always  thank you all so much for watching I'll  talk to you again soon
 

